141: const storyBeats=[
142: {date:'GOTHAM CITY · 1982',title:'A night at the movies',text:'<p>Gotham’s old theater empties into a rain-bright street. Thomas and Martha Wayne walk home with their young son, Bruce, still talking about the film. His mother holds his hand; his father listens as if the story matters as much as the night is beautiful.</p><p>Their shortcut passes a narrow alley. A stranger steps from the dark and asks for money. Thomas does not reach for a weapon or shout. He gives the gunman <strong>$200</strong>—everything he has—and tries to make himself the only danger in the alley.</p><p class="quote">“Take it. Just let my family go.”</p><p>For one suspended second, the city seems to hold its breath.</p>'},
143: {date:'GOTHAM CITY · THAT NIGHT',title:'The promise',text:'<p>The gunshot cracks across the street. Thomas and Martha fall beneath the streetlamp. Bruce is left kneeling beside them, surrounded by a city that keeps moving but will never look the same to him again.</p><p>In the weeks that follow, adults speak softly around him. They tell him he is safe now, that the police will find the killer, that time will make the pain smaller. None of those words can bring his parents back. Bruce learns that helplessness can feel louder than any siren.</p><p>Grief hardens into a promise—not a plan yet, only a vow he will carry into every room: <strong>no one else should have to feel this alone.</strong></p>'},
144: {date:'WAYNE MANOR · THE YEARS AFTER',title:'A house full of echoes',text:'<p>The manor is enormous, but after the funeral it feels too quiet. Bruce grows up among portraits, locked rooms, and the careful kindness of the family’s longtime butler, Alfred Pennyworth. Alfred makes breakfast, remembers birthdays, and never asks Bruce to pretend he is all right.</p><p>Bruce studies, travels, and searches for a way to understand the violence that took his parents. He learns how fear works, how people survive, and how easily a powerful family can mistake its own comfort for the health of its city.</p><p>The promise stays with him. It changes shape as he does: first a child’s wish, then a question, and finally a responsibility he cannot hand to anyone else.</p>'},
145: {date:'GOTHAM CITY · 2008',title:'A fortune, a house, a name',text:'<p>Twenty-six years later, Bruce uncovers a fortune his father had quietly set aside—seed money for a plan Thomas never finished. Bruce invests it, buys back a neglected mansion, and gives the work a public name: <strong>Wayne Enterprises.</strong></p><p>From the outside, it looks like a return to the life he was born to lead. The grand rooms fill with donors, champagne, and polished promises. Harvey Dent, Gotham’s tireless district attorney, is among the guests. He believes the city can be repaired through courage and honest institutions.</p><p>Bruce admires that conviction. He also knows how many people are waiting for someone else to fix things. Behind the parties, he begins asking harder questions about what a single person can do.</p>'},
146: {date:'GOTHAM CITY · 2009',title:'The cost of hope',text:'<p>A year later, a terrible incident leaves Harvey Dent’s face scarred and his life changed. The city’s bright public hope is suddenly vulnerable to pain, anger, and the cruel appetite of headlines. Bruce watches the damage from a distance and recognizes the same old feeling: the moment when safety proves it was never guaranteed.</p><p>He retreats from the parties and the easy explanations. With a bag in hand, he descends beneath Wayne Manor into a forgotten cave. The stone is cold, the passages are rough, and the darkness seems almost honest. Here, at least, nobody asks him to smile for a photograph.</p><p>For a while, Bruce tells himself solitude is the same thing as purpose.</p>'},
147: {date:'WAYNE CAVE · THE FIRST VISIT',title:'A man in the dark',text:'<p>“Excuse me, sir. Are you looking for something?” an old man calls from the cave entrance. “There’s nothing in here. It’s old—just like me.”</p><p>Bruce bristles. “I’m trying to do something with this place. What makes you think you can tell me what to do?” The stranger does not rise to the challenge. He simply points out that Bruce is standing on private property and waits for him to answer.</p><p>Bruce refuses to leave. The old man gives him one last look and walks away. The exchange is awkward, almost absurd, but it stays with Bruce. Someone has finally spoken to him plainly, without fear of his name or his money.</p>'},
148: {date:'WAYNE CAVE · THREE MONTHS LATER',title:'The long quiet',text:'<p>Days pass. Bruce returns to the cave with tools, then with plans, then with nothing but questions. He measures the cavern and marks out possible work areas. He tries to turn the rock into a headquarters by force of will, but every task exposes another one he does not know how to do.</p><p>Above him, the manor continues its routines. Below, the cave answers with drips of water and the occasional flutter of wings. Bruce begins to understand that a mission built alone may only become another way to hide.</p><p>One evening he hears footsteps at the entrance. The same old man has come back. This time, he is carrying a thermos and a small stack of notes.</p>'},
149: {date:'WAYNE CAVE · A RETURN',title:'An unexpected ally',text:'<p>“Excuse me, sir. Remember me?” The man sets down the thermos. “I’m Alfred.” Bruce has known him all his life, of course, but down here they meet differently: not as employer and servant, nor as grieving boy and patient guardian, but as two people looking at the same unfinished room.</p><p>Alfred has sketched a workbench, a computer station, and a safer route back to the house. He has also found a book on self-defense. Bruce studies the pages and recognizes how much he does not know. Alfred does not gloat. He pours tea.</p><p>“You’ll need more than determination, sir,” he says. “Determination is what gets you to the door. Preparation is what gets you home.”</p>'},
150: {date:'WAYNE CAVE · THE PLAN TAKES SHAPE',title:'Tools, not answers',text:'<p>They work in fits and starts. Alfred knows how to keep a project moving; Bruce knows how to ask questions that make the project bigger. The computer station becomes a place to sort reports and maps. A suit bay takes shape against the stone. The elevator connects the cave to the estate without sending either man through the front hall.</p><p>Every object has a purpose and a limit. Batarangs can distract or disarm, but they cannot make a bad decision wise. Smoke pellets can cover a retreat, but they cannot turn a retreat into a victory. A grapple prototype can bridge a gap only if the person using it thinks before leaping.</p><p>Bruce begins to see the cave not as a lair, but as a workshop for learning how to help.</p>'},
151: {date:'WAYNE CAVE · THE VEHICLE BAY',title:'A machine with sharp edges',text:'<p>The car is the most ambitious piece of the plan and the least finished. Its armored panels catch the work lights; its engine answers a test with a growl that makes dust fall from the ceiling. Alfred circles it with a clipboard, noting what still needs attention.</p><p>“It’s not quite street legal,” he says. “And I would avoid the red switch.” Bruce asks if it is safe. Alfred considers the question for a beat too long. They agree to call it a prototype and to keep the first test somewhere far from traffic.</p><p>The machine represents possibility, but also temptation: the urge to make an entrance instead of doing the patient work. Bruce closes the hood. Tonight, the car can wait.</p>'},
152: {date:'WAYNE CAVE · BEFORE DAWN',title:'The first lesson',text:'<p>Alfred lays the self-defense field guide on the workbench. It is not as impressive as the suit, the car, or the glowing computer screens. Its pages are worn at the corners, full of diagrams and notes in Alfred’s neat hand. Bruce opens it carefully.</p><p>“Begin with the book,” Alfred tells him. “Learn how to keep your balance. Learn when to move, when to listen, and when walking away is the bravest thing you can do.” Bruce looks toward the equipment waiting around the cave. For the first time, it feels less like a collection of answers and more like a list of responsibilities.</p><p>There is still a long way to go. But the work has finally begun.</p>'},
153: {date:'GOTHAM · CASE FILE 01',title:'The question in the dark',villain:'riddler',scene:'THE NARROWS',caption:'A QUESTION WITH NO ANSWER',text:`<p>A green question mark is painted on the shutter of a closed pawnshop. Bruce photographs it from across the street. It is not a confession—just the first hint that someone wants the city to know he is watching.</p><p>Alfred finds a string of riddles in old police reports. Bruce files them away, unsure whether the author is a criminal, a prankster, or both.</p>`},
154: {date:'GOTHAM · CASE FILE 02',title:'A smile on the wire',villain:'joker',scene:'GOTHAM BROADCAST',caption:'LAUGHTER AFTER MIDNIGHT',text:`<p>Every screen in a downtown electronics shop flickers to life at once. A pale face smiles through the static, telling a joke no one in the room finds funny. The broadcast ends with a burst of confetti and a list of abandoned buildings.</p><p>Bruce watches the recording twice. The danger is not the punchline. It is how carefully the speaker studied his audience.</p>`},
155: {date:'GOTHAM · CASE FILE 03',title:'The coin that never lands',villain:'twoface',scene:'OLD COURTHOUSE',caption:'JUSTICE ON BOTH SIDES',text:`<p>Harvey Dent once believed a fair trial could change Gotham. Now a scarred former prosecutor lets a coin decide which version of justice will speak for him. One side is bright; the other is dark and worn.</p><p>Bruce keeps Harvey’s old campaign button beside the case notes. He will not reduce his friend to the damage done to him.</p>`},
156: {date:'GOTHAM · CASE FILE 04',title:'Something beneath the river',villain:'croc',scene:'EAST END DRAINS',caption:'EYES BELOW THE WATER',text:`<p>Workers refuse to enter a storm tunnel after hearing heavy footsteps beneath the grates. A hulking figure is seen only in the ripples and the brief flash of yellow eyes.</p><p>The newspapers call him a monster. Bruce writes a different question in the margin: what happened to the boy before the city gave him that name?</p>`},
157: {date:'GOTHAM · CASE FILE 05',title:'The tea-party invitation',villain:'hatter',scene:'ABANDONED THEATER',caption:'ONE SEAT LEFT EMPTY',text:`<p>In a derelict playhouse, a tiny paper invitation waits on a velvet chair. It promises a perfect world to anyone willing to follow the White Rabbit. The ink is fresh; the theater has been empty for years.</p><p>Bruce recognizes the language of a trap dressed as comfort. Some people do not want power over a city. They want power over one person at a time.</p>`},
158: {date:'GOTHAM · CASE FILE 06',title:'The cold front',villain:'freeze',scene:'GOTHAM HARBOR',caption:'WINTER IN THE WRONG SEASON',text:`<p>A shipment of medical equipment disappears from a waterfront laboratory. Frost feathers the inside of a locked storage door, though the night outside is warm.</p><p>The lead points to Victor Fries, a scientist trying to keep his wife alive. Bruce marks the name carefully. A desperate motive does not make dangerous methods harmless.</p>`},
159: {date:'GOTHAM · CASE FILE 07',title:'The man who broke a wall',villain:'bane',scene:'BLACKGATE PRISON',caption:'STRENGTH IS A STRATEGY',text:`<p>A transfer report describes a prisoner who studies every guard rotation and every weakness in the building. Bane does not waste strength; he saves it for the moment it matters.</p><p>Bruce adds endurance drills to his training. Winning a fight, he realizes, may begin long before the first blow.</p>`},
160: {date:'GOTHAM · CASE FILE 08',title:'The clown’s calling card',villain:'joker',scene:'MONTOYA CIRCUS',caption:'THE SHOW WILL GO ON',text:`<p>A shuttered circus tent is found ringed with playing cards. Somewhere inside, a music box turns by itself. The officers who enter hear applause from an empty grandstand.</p><p>Bruce studies the scene photographs. The performer wants fear, but he also wants an audience. That need may be the most dangerous clue of all.</p>`},
161: {date:'GOTHAM · CASE FILE 09',title:'A city-sized puzzle',villain:'riddler',scene:'FINANCIAL DISTRICT',caption:'FOLLOW THE GREEN LIGHT',text:`<p>Traffic signals across three blocks change in a sequence that spells out a riddle. The pattern leads to a locked service room—and a second riddle hidden inside it.</p><p>Bruce maps the route without entering the buildings. Whoever designed the game is testing patience as much as intelligence.</p>`},
162: {date:'GOTHAM · CASE FILE 10',title:'Harvey’s other face',villain:'twoface',scene:'GOTHAM CENTRAL',caption:'A COIN IN THE EVIDENCE ROOM',text:`<p>A witness refuses to testify after receiving a silver coin with a scorched edge. Harvey’s old colleagues want to protect him from the investigation; Harvey would have demanded the truth.</p><p>Bruce asks Alfred to keep the original case files safe. Memory can preserve a person’s best self, even when the present tries to erase it.</p>`},
