  Line 213: {date:'GOTHAM CITY · THAT NIGHT',title:'The promise',text:'<p>The gunshot cracks across the street. Thomas and Martha fall beneath the streetlamp. Bruce is left kneeling beside them, surrounded by a city that keeps moving but will never look the same to him again.</p><p>In the weeks that follow, adults speak softly around him. They tell him he is safe now, that the police will find the killer, that time will make the pain smaller. None of those words can bring his parents back. Bruce learns that helplessness can feel louder than any siren.</p><p>Grief hardens into a promise—not a plan yet, only a vow he will carry into every room: <strong>no one else should have to feel this alone.</strong></p>'},

  Line 214: {date:'WAYNE MANOR · THE YEARS AFTER',title:'A house full of echoes',text:'<p>The manor is enormous, but after the funeral it feels too quiet. Bruce grows up among portraits, locked rooms, and the careful kindness of the family’s longtime butler, Alfred Pennyworth. Alfred makes breakfast, remembers birthdays, and never asks Bruce to pretend he is all right.</p><p>Bruce studies, travels, and searches for a way to understand the violence that took his parents. He learns how fear works, how people survive, and how easily a powerful family can mistake its own comfort for the health of its city.</p><p>The promise stays with him. It changes shape as he does: first a child’s wish, then a question, and finally a responsibility he cannot hand to anyone else.</p>'},

  Line 216: {date:'GOTHAM CITY · 2009',title:'The cost of hope',text:'<p>A year later, a terrible incident leaves Harvey Dent’s face scarred and his life changed. The city’s bright public hope is suddenly vulnerable to pain, anger, and the cruel appetite of headlines. Bruce watches the damage from a distance and recognizes the same old feeling: the moment when safety proves it was never guaranteed.</p><p>He retreats from the parties and the easy explanations. With a bag in hand, he descends beneath Wayne Manor into a forgotten cave. The stone is cold, the passages are rough, and the darkness seems almost honest. Here, at least, nobody asks him to smile for a photograph.</p><p>For a while, Bruce tells himself solitude is the same thing as purpose.</p>'},

  Line 218: {date:'WAYNE CAVE · THREE MONTHS LATER',title:'The long quiet',text:'<p>Days pass. Bruce returns to the cave with tools, then with plans, then with nothing but questions. He measures the cavern and marks out possible work areas. He tries to turn the rock into a headquarters by force of will, but every task exposes another one he does not know how to do.</p><p>Above him, the manor continues its routines. Below, the cave answers with drips of water and the occasional flutter of wings. Bruce begins to understand that a mission built alone may only become another way to hide.</p><p>One evening he hears footsteps at the entrance. The same old man has come back. This time, he is carrying a thermos and a small stack of notes.</p>'},

  Line 221: {date:'WAYNE CAVE · THE VEHICLE BAY',title:'A machine with sharp edges',text:'<p>The car is the most ambitious piece of the plan and the least finished. Its armored panels catch the work lights; its engine answers a test with a growl that makes dust fall from the ceiling. Alfred circles it with a clipboard, noting what still needs attention.</p><p>“It’s not quite street legal,” he says. “And I would avoid the red switch.” Bruce asks if it is safe. Alfred considers the question for a beat too long. They agree to call it a prototype and to keep the first test somewhere far from traffic.</p><p>The machine represents possibility, but also temptation: the urge to make an entrance instead of doing the patient work. Bruce closes the hood. Tonight, the car can wait.</p>'},

  Line 222: {date:'WAYNE CAVE · BEFORE DAWN',title:'The first lesson',text:'<p>Alfred lays the self-defense field guide on the workbench. It is not as impressive as the suit, the car, or the glowing computer screens. Its pages are worn at the corners, full of diagrams and notes in Alfred’s neat hand. Bruce opens it carefully.</p><p>“Begin with the book,” Alfred tells him. “Learn how to keep your balance. Learn when to move, when to listen, and when walking away is the bravest thing you can do.” Bruce looks toward the equipment waiting around the cave. For the first time, it feels less like a collection of answers and more like a list of responsibilities.</p><p>There is still a long way to go. But the work has finally begun.</p>'},

  Line 224: {date:'GOTHAM · CASE FILE 02',title:'A smile on the wire',villain:'joker',scene:'GOTHAM BROADCAST',caption:'LAUGHTER AFTER MIDNIGHT',text:`<p>Every screen in a downtown electronics shop flickers to life at once. A pale face smiles through the static, telling a joke no one in the room finds funny. The broadcast ends with a burst of confetti and a list of abandoned buildings.</p><p>Bruce watches the recording twice. The danger is not the punchline. It is how carefully the speaker studied his audience.</p>`},

  Line 225: {date:'GOTHAM · CASE FILE 03',title:'The coin that never lands',villain:'twoface',scene:'OLD COURTHOUSE',caption:'JUSTICE ON BOTH SIDES',text:`<p>Harvey Dent once believed a fair trial could change Gotham. Now a scarred former prosecutor lets a coin decide which version of justice will speak for him. One side is bright; the other is dark and worn.</p><p>Bruce keeps Harvey’s old campaign button beside the case notes. He will not reduce his friend to the damage done to him.</p>`},

  Line 228: {date:'GOTHAM · CASE FILE 06',title:'The cold front',villain:'freeze',scene:'GOTHAM HARBOR',caption:'WINTER IN THE WRONG SEASON',text:`<p>A shipment of medical equipment disappears from a waterfront laboratory. Frost feathers the inside of a locked storage door, though the night outside is warm.</p><p>The lead points to Victor Fries, a scientist trying to keep his wife alive. Bruce marks the name carefully. A desperate motive does not make dangerous methods harmless.</p>`},

  Line 230: {date:'GOTHAM · CASE FILE 08',title:'The clown’s calling card',villain:'joker',scene:'MONTOYA CIRCUS',caption:'THE SHOW WILL GO ON',text:`<p>A shuttered circus tent is found ringed with playing cards. Somewhere inside, a music box turns by itself. The officers who enter hear applause from an empty grandstand.</p><p>Bruce studies the scene photographs. The performer wants fear, but he also wants an audience. That need may be the most dangerous clue of all.</p>`},

  Line 236: {date:'GOTHAM · CASE FILE 14',title:'Bane’s careful silence',villain:'bane',scene:'PORT ADAMS',caption:'THE STRONGEST MAN WAITS',text:`<p>A crate of experimental medical supplies vanishes from the port. The theft is clean, with no broken locks and no witnesses willing to talk. Bane has made the city look in the wrong direction.</p><p>Bruce studies the timing. A patient opponent can turn a city’s routines into a weapon.</p>`},

  Line 239: {date:'GOTHAM · CASE FILE 17',title:'The courthouse divided',villain:'twoface',scene:'GOTHAM COURTHOUSE',caption:'ONE VERDICT, TWO OUTCOMES',text:`<p>Harvey sends a sealed envelope to Bruce. Inside is a list of people he wants protected from his own gang. The handwriting shifts from careful to jagged halfway down the page.</p><p>Bruce does not mistake the letter for an apology. He sees it as a chance to keep innocent people out of the crossfire.</p>`},

  Line 243: {date:'GOTHAM · CASE FILE 21',title:'A test of endurance',villain:'bane',scene:'NARROWS ROOFLINE',caption:'NO SHORTCUT THROUGH FEAR',text:`<p>Bane leaves a trail of staged emergencies across the Narrows. Each one forces responders to split their attention. The trap is not a single fight; it is exhaustion.</p><p>Bruce changes the plan: call in help, rotate teams, and refuse to let pride set the pace. The city is stronger when no one has to carry it alone.</p>`},

  Line 245: {date:'GOTHAM · CASE FILE 23',title:'The answer beneath the question',villain:'riddler',scene:'WAYNE STATION',caption:'EVERY CLUE LEAVES A TRACE',text:`<p>A final riddle points to a substation carrying power to a hospital. Bruce spots the hidden override before the lights go out and alerts the engineers.</p><p>The puzzle is solved, but the lesson is larger: cleverness without care can become another kind of cruelty.</p>`},

